\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 British decolonisation between 1945 and 1968 was mainly caused by pressure from nationalist movements.' Assess the validity of this view. [25 marks]\par
\par
Nationalist movements were certainly a significant cause of decolonisation between 1945 and 1968 and growing national conscientiousness was a very real phenomenon throughout Asia and Africa in these decades. However, the influence of nationalism can be overstated; nationalists did not, by bthemselves, pose an existential threat. Rather, their apparent success was enabled by a range of factors quite independent of the nationalists\rquote  control. The shifting attitudes of the British themselves, supported by Britain\rquote s changing economic interests, encouraged the process of decolonisation. Moreover, it was the enormous geopolitical upheavals of the mid-20th century that determined decolonisation happened when it did.\par
\par
The influence of nationalists can certainly be detected in a range of colonial settings. Most notably, the nationalist movement in India (the Indian National Congress), led by charismatic figures such as Gandhi and Nehru, had completely transformed the fortunes of the British Raj. The civil disobedience movements of the 1930s, culminating in the Quit India campaign of 1942 had forced the British to recognise that they could not continue governing India indefinitely; by 1947 so much power had already been conceded that \lquote dominion status\rquote  was more a recognition of reality than a significant concession. Similarly, the power of Aung San\rquote s nationalist movement in neighbouring Burma meant that the British were never able to reassert full control there after the defeat of Japan in 1945. The competing, and increasingly violent, forces of Zionism and Arab nationalism in Palestine made it impossible to maintain control there by 1948. Indeed, Arab nationalism had long challenged British hegemony in the Middle East, culminating in Nasser\rquote s humiliation of Britain in 1956 when the Suez Canal was nationalised. By the 1950s Greek Cypriots had formed a guerrilla organisation (EOKA) campaigning for union with Greece. A state of emergency was declared and 25,000 British troops were mobilized. The Prime Minister, Harold Macmillan, was well aware that the strength of nationalism could not be contained by force and granted independence in 1960. Faced with multiple nationalist movements asserting themselves after 1945, it was clear that the British Empire faced internal threats on a scale never seen before.\par
\par
Moreover, the years following the Second World War saw the growth of nationalist movements in Africa too. Urbanisation, growing literacy, and fitful economic development had encouraged the growth of a new English-speaking African elite. This elite was able to foster a sense of national identity that had not hitherto existed within African colonies\rquote  artificial borders. This was noted and accepted at the time by Macmillan in his \lquote Wind of Change\rquote  speech in Cape Town in 1960. In the Gold Coast, Kwame Nkrumah was able to rally a convincing majority of his countrymen behind his Convention People\rquote s Party which left the British with little option than to hand over power to him in 1957. Similarly, in Tanzania the colonial authorities felt that it was a better option to collaborate with nationalist leader, Julius Nyerere, than resist him indefinitely. Indeed, the miserable experiences of French resistance to nationalism in Indo-China and Algeria in the 1950s left the British government with little enthusiasm for entering similar protracted struggles in their own empire. The violence from nationalists in Nyasaland in 1959 helped convince ministers such as Iain Macleod that a swift deal with nationalists, followed by a hasty exit, was preferable to a bloody confrontation.\par
\par
Yet, it is important to qualify the role of nationalist movements in causing decolonisation. Nationalism was not powerful everywhere. In Nigeria, for example, it proved difficult to create any coherent and popular national identity in a country comprising 250 languages, where \lquote millions lived and died without knowing that they were Nigerians, let alone subjects of the British Empire\rquote  (Brendon). As Hobsbawm has argued, \lquote the idea of an independent sovereign state which we take for granted, was meaningless to [most] people.\rquote  Even Kwame Nkrumah was inconsistent in his nationalism, at various points embracing the competing, internationalist ideologies of Marxism and pan-Africanism. The violence that the British faced in their colonies was often spontaneous and ambiguous. The Mau Mau uprising in Kenya, for instance, whilst certainly anti-colonial, had little sense of a clear \lquote national\rquote  objective, and the nationalist leader, Jomo Kenyatta, spent a good deal of time trying to distance himself from it. Moreover, in Malaya the British faced an insurgency from 1948 in the shape of the Malayan Communist Party, a largely Chinese group that was shunned by mainstream Malay nationalists. Indeed, in both these cases the British were able to defeat the insurgents militarily with (relative) ease, and so avoided being chased out by violence. Furthermore, in many instances, the British willingly courted nationalists as potential new collaborators through whom they could continue to wield influence as members of the Commonwealth. This was the case in Nigeria where, by 1960, the British were desperate to cultivate nationalists to whom responsibility could be passed. Nationalist movements, therefore, cannot be seen as the only cause of decolonisation everywhere. \par
\par
Rather, events in the metropole need also to be considered in explaining British decolonisation between 1945 and 1968. There had long been a significant group within Britain who had been sceptical of imperialism. But after the Second World War the moral repugnance that some felt towards empire became much more widespread in the aftermath of the struggle against Nazism; the concepts of racial superiority which underlay imperialism became deeply unfashionable in the post-war world. This can be seen in the outcry over the treatment of internees at the Hola Camp in Kenya in 1959; liberalism and democracy were the values with which the British self-identified, and imperialism (and the violence often associated with it) was incompatible with these values. Indeed, decolonisation seems to have been a remarkably uncontroversial issue in British politics; no general election hinged on imperial matters, and there was a broad cross-party consensus about the general trajectory. After the Suez Crisis, the Conservative Party ditched its previous attachment to empire and, under Macmillan and Macleod, set about dismantling the greater part of Britain\rquote s African empire. As Porter has argued convincingly, apathy and ignorance partly explain this; a 1948 survey revealing that half the British population were unable to name even a single colony is particularly revealing. The minority who were unhappy with decolonisation were able to console themselves with the idea that the Commonwealth might prove a lasting tool of British prestige around the world.\par
\par
This changing attitude in Britain was encouraged by the country\rquote s shifting economic interests. Britain\rquote s economy had been ravaged by the Second World War and it was heavily dependent on US loans and Marshall Aid. After 1945 the voting public were more interested in public spending on the welfare state. Strategic thinkers in government, meanwhile, wanted to focus spending on nuclear weapons to face the Cold War threat from the USSR. This left little money for dealing with colonial matters, particularly if the colonies were experiencing upheaval, counter-insurgency being an expensive activity. Notwithstanding the dollars earned from Malaya\rquote s rubber and tin, economic development in Britain\rquote s tropical empire proved disappointing. This was most obviously demonstrated in the abortive Tanganyika Groundnuts Scheme (1946-51) which cost \'a336 million, stirred up resentment amongst the native population, and yielded virtually no groundnuts. By 1965 British imports from the Commonwealth and empire had slumped from 44.9% of the total in 1948, to 29.8%. Exports had similarly decreased from 46.1% to 27.9%. Meanwhile, imports from Europe had increased from 20.5% in 1948 to 30.6% in 1965, with exports to Europe similarly increasing from 24.8% to 32.5% as the European economies experienced a post-war boom. It seemed clear where Britain\rquote s commercial destiny lay, and this explains Britain\rquote s applications to join the European Economic Community in 1963 and 1967. From an economic point of view, the continuance of Britain\rquote s imperial commitments made little sense. In this sense, the actions of nationalist movements had little bearing on the overall direction of colonial policy.\par
\par
Nonetheless, it was the changing geopolitical context that actually determined the timing of decolonisation. The Second World War broke the strength of the empire as, between Dunkirk in 1940 and the fall of Singapore in 1942, Britain fell into what Darwin has termed \lquote the strategic abyss\rquote . The war didn\rquote t just devastate Britain\rquote s economy, it also destroyed its prestige in the eyes of its indigenous peoples. This was particularly true in Asia where the rapid Japanese advances into Malaya and Burma humiliated the British and made it impossible to convincingly re-establish the status quo ante. The contingencies of war meant that Churchill\rquote s government felt forced to make desperate promises about self-government to Indians (in the 1942 Cripps Mission) and the Americans (in the 1941 Atlantic Charter). Britain\rquote s self-governing dominions, Australia, New Zealand and Canada, long an important pillar of British power, realised that their economic and security interests were better served by drawing closer to the United States. As has been established, the imperialisms of Germany and Japan also tarnished the concept beyond redemption making it difficult to justify imperialism after 1945. Despite victory, it is no coincidence that the war was followed by a rush of decolonisation in the late 1940s.\par
\par
The post-war world was then dominated by two nominally anti-imperial powers, the USA and the USSR. Britain\rquote s geopolitical weakness relative to the United States was dramatically revealed during the Suez Crisis of 1956 when the US refused to condone Britain and France\rquote s invasion of Egypt. Exploiting Britain\rquote s economic vulnerability, the US refused to support sterling until British forces were withdrawn, resulting in Britain\rquote s humiliation and the end of any hopes for continued  hegemony in the region. The fear that continued imperial dominance might alienate the Arab world, pushing them into the arms of the Soviet bloc, meant the Americans were determined not to prop up European colonialism. The fear of the USSR exploiting nationalism could also be seen in Africa when Belgian rule in the Congo collapsed in 1960, leaving the USSR hoping to exploit the grievances that were unleashed in the ensuing catastrophe. The pressures of the Cold War concentrated minds in London and Washington, forcing the British to realise that they needed to maintain good relations with their colonies (and the nationalists within them), or else risk losing important alliances.\par
\par
Nationalist movements, therefore, were fortunate in that they were pushing at a door that was already opening by the mid-20th century. Britain\rquote s economic and domestic needs meant that the metropole was inclined to countenance decolonisation where before this had been unthinkable. Moreover, it was the changing international context that ensured this process happened rapidly between 1945 and 1968, as British power struggled to assert itself in the face of its rivals. Nationalists certainly helped shape the process of decolonisation, and in some colonies were more influential than others. But the overall direction of travel was driven by forces outside of their control.\par
